<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">13 November 1913 · Hartford, Connecticut</p><h1 class="sp-title">Emmeline Pankhurst: Freedom or Death</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmeline-pankhurst">Emmeline Pankhurst</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>Emmeline Pankhurst, founder of the Women's Social and Political Union, had led the British suffragettes from marches and heckling to window-smashing and arson as Parliament repeatedly failed to give women the vote. Released from prison under the 'Cat and Mouse' Act, she toured the United States to raise money and to explain the militant campaign.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>I am here as a soldier who has temporarily left the field of battle in order to explain... what civil war is like when civil war is waged by women.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Speech at Hartford, Connecticut, 13 November 1913.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Freedom_or_death" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Wikisource</span></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>When war broke out in 1914 the WSPU suspended its campaign and supported the war effort. The Representation of the People Act of 1918 gave the vote to many women over thirty, and in 1928, weeks after Pankhurst's death, women won the vote on the same terms as men.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/representation-of-the-people-act-1918">Representation of the People Act 1918</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equal-franchise-act-1928">Equal Franchise Act 1928</a></div></div></section>
